# Mark the tracked binaries explicitly.
#
# Git already detects these as binary by heuristic, so this changes no stored content - it just
# stops git guessing. Two things it buys us:
#   - no CRLF conversion is ever attempted on them (core.autocrlf=true is set on the Windows box)
#   - a conflict on a DLL is reported as a plain binary conflict instead of git trying to merge it
#
# The compiled DLLs in Assemblies/ are committed on purpose - RimWorld loads them directly and never
# compiles anything, so checking out the repo on a second machine has to yield a working mod without
# a build step. The cost is that DLLs cannot be merged: two machines building the same source produce
# different bytes. Always rebuild before committing C#, and always fetch before starting work.
#
# Deliberately NOT setting "* text=auto" here: that would renormalise every existing text file's line
# endings in one commit, producing a repo-wide diff that buries real history.

# Shell scripts MUST keep LF endings. core.autocrlf=true on the Windows box would otherwise check
# them out with CRLF, and bash on the Linux box then fails with "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env
# bash^M" - the script would be broken the moment it arrived, having looked fine on Windows.
*.sh text eol=lf

*.dll binary
*.pdb binary
*.dds binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.wav binary
*.ogg binary
*.rws binary
